{pad:meta title='Layouts: extends, block and parent'/}

<p>The directories give a page its frame - every <code>_inits.pad</code> and <code>_exits.pad</code> on the way to it. A layout is a frame the page picks itself, wherever it lives, and whose regions it can override one by one. This is <code>_layouts/report.pad</code>:</p>

{app:source '_layouts/report.pad'/}

<p>A page that extends it fills in the title, adds to the sidebar and leaves the footer as it is:</p>

{common:example 'fragments/layouts_1'/}

<h2>The three tags</h2>

<ul>
  <li><code>{pad:open/}pad:extends '_layouts/report'/{pad:close/}</code> - the page is framed by that layout instead of by its directories' wrappers. The layout is named from the application root, like a <code>{pad:open/}pad:page{pad:close/}</code>; an underscore directory keeps it from being a page of its own. A layout may extend another layout.</li>
  <li><code>{pad:open/}pad:block 'name'{pad:close/}...{pad:open/}/pad:block{pad:close/}</code> in the layout is a region: its content renders unless the page overrides it. The same pair standing directly in the page is an override. The name is always quoted.</li>
  <li><code>{pad:open/}pad:parent{pad:close/}</code> inside an override is the content it overrides - to add to a region instead of replacing it.</li>
</ul>

<p>What the page holds outside its blocks goes where the layout writes {app:construct 'page'/}, or in front of the layout when it has none.</p>

<h2>Blocks in the directory wrappers</h2>

<p>The blocks of an ordinary <code>_inits.pad</code> are regions too, so a page that extends nothing still overrides them: with <code>&lt;title&gt;{pad:open/}pad:block 'title'{pad:close/}My site{pad:open/}/pad:block{pad:close/}&lt;/title&gt;</code> in the wrapper, a page sets its own title with <code>{pad:open/}pad:block 'title'{pad:close/}Sales{pad:open/}/pad:block{pad:close/}</code> - no PHP needed. A block of such a page that overrides nothing renders where it stands.</p>

<h2>Resolved before rendering</h2>

<p>Blocks are put together in the text while the page is assembled, before anything renders, so they cost nothing at run time. Under the strict check a block of an extending page that overrides nothing is reported - usually a misspelt name - and so is an <code>{pad:open/}pad:extends{pad:close/}</code> inside another tag, where the assembly does not look.</p>
